# {{ .name }}

Run-to-completion .NET job on the Intropy job host. One run = one call of
`Job.ExecuteAsync`, then exit. Scheduling lives outside the job — the cadence
is deployment configuration (a Kubernetes CronJob).

## How a run flows

```
scheduled run (CronJob)
   │
   ▼
JobRunner: wait for the Dapr sidecar ──▶ timeout ──▶ exit 2
   │
   ▼
Job.ExecuteAsync, inside the run's span ──▶ throws, or Failed > 0 ──▶ exit 1
   │
   ▼
RunSummary (processed / failed / skipped)
   │
   ▼
JobRunner: shut the sidecar down ──▶ exit 0
```

SIGTERM and Ctrl+C cancel the run, which exits `0`: the remaining work is left
for the next run, so the job must be safe to repeat.

## Build and test

```bash
task build
task test
```

`task test` runs both test projects (`task test:unit` / `task test:integration`
run them separately). The unit suite covers the job's own logic without a
host. The integration suite proves the DI graph builds without a sidecar, and
runs the job once under the real `JobRunner` with the edges faked — the Dapr
sidecar (`DaprClient`, via NSubstitute) and the two platform-service clients
(from `Intropy.Framework.Testing`) — to pin the exit-code contract. `task
coverage` runs the integration suite with branch coverage (Cobertura report
under the integration project's `TestResults/`).

## Container image

```bash
docker build -t {{ .appId }} .
```

Multi-stage build on the .NET 10 chiseled runtime. The image contains only the
app — the Dapr sidecar and components come from the deployment environment.
